WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.440
Welcome to unintended consequences, Episode 6, for May 11th, 2026.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.500
Starting the week with a case study, we'll be thinking about for days,

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.840
the Strison effect. In the spring of 2003,

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.240
Barbara Strison filed a $50 million lawsuit in Los Angeles Superior Court.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:25.560
Her target was a single aerial photograph of her Clifftop Malibu residents.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.220
The image formed one small part of a much larger collection.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.820
A photographer named Kenneth Adelman had captured 12,000 pictures along the California coast.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:42.080
His goal was to document shoreline erosion for scientific and policy use.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:48.300
Before the legal papers arrived, that particular photo had been viewed only six times.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.500
Barbara Strison wanted to protect her private life from constant public scrutiny.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:59.420
By the early 2000s, high-resolution digital cameras and the expanding internet

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:04.100
made it simple for images of celebrity's homes to spread without permission.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:09.540
Security concerns and questions of personal boundaries felt real and immediate.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.660
Her legal team presented the photo as an invasion of privacy rather than a neutral scientific record.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:23.120
They argued that even a coastal survey could be repurposed by tabloids or fans.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:29.500
At the time, courts were only beginning to consider how old privacy laws applied to online pictures.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.720
Many public figures had already used quiet legal steps to keep unwanted images offline.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.120
Strison therefore followed what looked like a standard and reasonable approach.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:46.800
She turned to the legal system to assert control before wider circulation could occur.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.880
Kenneth Adelman had launched the California Coastal Records Project in 2002.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.680
The project created a public archive of the state's 870-mile shoreline.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:06.920
Every image sat on a plain website with no paywalls and no special tags highlighting famous residents.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:11.220
Strison attorneys served notice in early 2003.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.380
They asked for the photo to be deleted and for damages tied to alleged trespass and privacy

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:21.160
choose. The case moved through ordinary civil procedures.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.980
Strison's side stressed the commercial value of her likeness and the risk of misuse.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.620
The filing carried no public campaign, the aim was quiet containment through the courts.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:37.340
Once the lawsuit became known, the opposite of containment took place.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.740
Tech journalist Mike Maznick later gave the pattern a name in 2005.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.460
He called it the Streisand effect.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.980
Within weeks, links to the disputed photograph traveled across early blogs,

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:58.960
message boards, and news sites. The image that had been seen six times reached more than 420,000

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:03.500
page views by the end of the month. Adelman kept the photo in place.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:10.460
He noted that it remained part of a systematic coastal survey rather than any targeted expose.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.560
The legal action itself supplied the missing context.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:21.780
A celebrity address plus public controversy turned a dry environmental database into a story worth

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.420
sharing. News outlets that rarely covered coastal erosion ran pieces on the case.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.960
Search engines indexed the image more prominently.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:35.820
Copies of the photo appeared on servers beyond California jurisdiction.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:42.700
Strison lost the suit in 2003 and was ordered to cover Adelman's legal costs.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:48.460
The photograph continued to circulate as an emblem of the exposure she had sought to avoid.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.920
The California court left the image online and created no new rule limiting public coastal photography.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.300
Streisand moved on to other projects. In later years, the Streisand effect was cited in cases

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:06.460
involving corporate takedown notices and government internet blocks.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:13.000
Each instance showed the same pattern. The original coastal archive needed no redesign.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:16.960
The project simply continued its documentation work.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.460
Today, the photograph remains viewable on the original site,

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:25.860
now paired with a short note about the lawsuit that amplified it.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:32.340
Attempts to erase information in an open network often function as advertisements for that same

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:38.440
information. The act of suppression draws attention and creates new distribution channels.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:45.100
When the cost of sharing sits near zero, any controversy supplies both the motivation

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.020
and the links that algorithms and human curiosity then amplify.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:57.980
Decision makers facing unwanted visibility today still face the same trade-off Streisand faced.

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:04.300
They must weigh the uncertain chance of successful removal against the near certainty that the effort

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:10.760
itself will be noticed and redistributed. The durable question is therefore not whether suppression

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:17.300
is morally justified. It is whether the technical and social architecture of the medium makes

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:23.900
containment feasible in the first place. The same tension appears when companies today issue legal

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:30.400
demands to remove critical posts from public forums. What signals would you watch for that an attempt

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:36.940
a removal might instead widen the audience? That's unintended consequences for today.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:42.680
If this episode gave you something to think about, share it with a friend who'd appreciate the

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:47.740
story. Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll be back tomorrow with another case.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:55.460
